{
  "description": "Formulate a MILP for integrated train rescheduling and speed management under partial segment blockage. Variables: binary route choice r_{t,s} (train t uses side s of segment), binary order o_{i,j} (train i precedes j on shared track), continuous arrival a_{t,n}, departure d_{t,n} at station n, travel time τ_{t,seg} = (distance/v) approximated by piecewise linear speed profile with binary selection of speed level k: x_{t,seg,k}. Objective: minimize weighted sum of total delay Σ_t (a_{t,last}-a^0_{t,last}) and energy consumption Σ_t Σ_seg Σ_k e_{k} * x_{t,seg,k}. Constraints: (1) timetable feasibility: d_{t,n}=a_{t,n}+dw_{t,n}. (2) travel time linking: a_{t,n+1}=d_{t,n}+τ_{t,seg}. (3) speed profile linearization: τ_{t,seg}= Σ_k (dist_{seg}/v_k) * x_{t,seg,k}, Σ_k x_{t,seg,k}=1. (4) capacity: for any two trains i,j sharing a segment side s, enforce either i before j or j before i using o_{i,j}. Use big-M with arrival times and binary o_{i,j}. (5) blockage: during disruption window [T_start,T_end] on segment seg*, only one side allowed: r_{t,0}+r_{t,1}=1, and if r_{t,s}=1 then train must use side s; side s unavailable if blocked. (6) binary route choice linking to side usage: if r_{t,s}=1 then train uses side s on blocked segment, else uses normal side. (7) non‑negativity and binary definitions. Assumptions: piecewise constant speed levels, equal dwell times, big‑M values large enough, disruption only affects one segment. Provide full MILP formulation."
}

